// Files. `String::readFile(path, limit)` is the file at `path` as a String of up to `limit` bytes (the empty string when there is no such file),
// `String::fileSize(path)` is its size or -1, and `s.writeFile(path)` makes the file hold the bytes of `s` and gives 0, or -1 when it cannot be
// written. Like the input and the arguments they are not known to the validator beyond their range, so a program tests what it relies on.
// This copies `in.txt` to `out.txt` in capitals, and returns the size of the copy, or -1 if there is no `in.txt` (and then it writes nothing)
// or `out.txt` could not be written. Run it in a directory that has an `in.txt`.

if String::fileSize("in.txt") < 0 {
    return 0 - 1;
};

text = String::readFile("in.txt", 100000);

i = 0;
n = size(text);
while i < n [{ affirm i >= 0; affirm i <= n; affirm size(text) = n; }] shrinks n - i {
    c = toInt(get(text, i));
    if c >= 97 {
        if c <= 122 {
            text = set(text, i, toByte(c - 32));
        };
    };
    i = i + 1;
};

if text.writeFile("out.txt") < 0 {
    return 0 - 1;
};
return size(text);
